# en_wordlist.combined.gz

The English word list from the Android Open Source Project's LatinIME keyboard.

- Source: https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/inputmethods/LatinIME/+/refs/heads/main/dictionaries/en_wordlist.combined.gz
- Retrieved: 2026-08-19
- Header: `dictionary=main:en,locale=en,description=English,date=1414726273,version=54`
- Entries: 165,544 words, each with a 0-255 frequency and its own casing
- Licence: Apache License 2.0, Copyright (C) The Android Open Source Project

The list is vendored rather than downloaded at build time so
`engine/tools/gen-wordrank.py` reproduces `Resources/wordrank.bin` offline.

The 2014 date is a known limit: the list has `iPhone` and `Mateo`, and it does
not have `Jaxon` or `Malia`.

# count_2w.txt.gz

Peter Norvig's two-word counts, the quarter-million most frequent lowercase
bigrams of the Google Web Trillion Word Corpus.

- Source: https://norvig.com/ngrams/count_2w.txt
- Retrieved: 2026-09-11
- Entries: 286,358 bigrams with counts, none below 100,000. Contractions are
  split into two tokens ("don t"), so no pair contains an apostrophe
- Provenance, as stated at https://norvig.com/ngrams/: "Data files are derived
  from the Google Web Trillion Word Corpus, as described by Thorsten Brants and
  Alex Franz, and distributed by the Linguistic Data Consortium." Norvig
  publishes the count files openly on that page; the code beside them is MIT.

The file is vendored so `engine/tools/gen-wordpairs.py` reproduces
`Resources/wordpairs.bin` offline. Web text is the known limit: the counts hold
"bad ass" at 204,647 and never saw a text message.

# us-first-names.txt

US first names: every name that reached the Social Security Administration's
top 1,000 for a year and sex, 1880 to 2008. 6,782 names.

- Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hadley/data-baby-names/master/baby-names.csv
  (a mirror of the SSA tables; ssa.gov refused the direct download on
  2026-09-11)
- Retrieved: 2026-09-11
- Licence: US government data, public domain
- Order: highest single-year share first

`engine/tools/wordlist.py` appends the names the AOSP list does not carry
(2,961 of them, Nathen, Jesica and Danial among them) after the texting
lexicon, so a name never outranks a common word at a distance tie. Top-1000
is the known limit: Kylian and Kaius are not in it.

# ssa-names.tsv

Every US first name in the Social Security Administration's national baby
names files (`yobYYYY.txt`, 1880 to 2024), both sexes summed: births since
1980 and births all time. 104,819 names.

- Source: the SSA files at https://www.ssa.gov/oact/babynames/names.zip
  (ssa.gov refuses non-browser downloads; retrieved through the mirror
  https://github.com/dcadata/name-finder, `data/names/`, on 2026-09-12)
- Licence: US government work, public domain (17 U.S.C. 105); CC0 on
  https://catalog.data.gov/dataset/baby-names-from-social-security-card-applications-national-data
- Aggregated by `python3 eval/words/candidates.py --aggregate <dir>`

Feeds the dictionary loop (`specs/autocorrect-loop.md` units 1 and 2): a name
enters the word list only after the stock keyboard leaves it as typed.
`us-first-names.txt` (top-1000 per year to 2008) stays as the list the engine
already ships; this file is the wider candidate pool.

# wikidata-brands.tsv

English labels of Wikidata items that are a business (Q4830453), brand
(Q431289), public company (Q891723), online service (Q7094076), franchise
(Q1668024) or software (Q7889), with 15 or more sitelinks, kept where the label
is one ASCII token of three or more characters. 1,841 labels with their
sitelink count.

- Source: https://query.wikidata.org/sparql, retrieved 2026-09-12. Query:
  `SELECT DISTINCT ?label (MAX(?links) AS ?n) WHERE { VALUES ?cls { wd:Q4830453
  wd:Q431289 wd:Q891723 wd:Q7094076 wd:Q1668024 wd:Q7889 } ?item wdt:P31 ?cls ;
  wikibase:sitelinks ?links ; rdfs:label ?label . FILTER(LANG(?label)="en")
  FILTER(?links >= 15) } GROUP BY ?label ORDER BY DESC(?n) LIMIT 20000`
- Licence: CC0 (https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing). CC0 waives
  copyright, not trademark; the list names brands, it does not use them.

Feeds the dictionary loop the same way: a brand enters the word list only after
stock leaves it as typed. The noise a class query carries ("email" is an
instance of online service) is what the stock filter is for.
